### Blocking evidence and preservation rules owned by W-096

W-096 owns `opus.red_assertion` (block), implemented in the evidence rule
module. Scope it to W-096's seven numbered behaviours, in building,
verifying, review and done; do not retroactively reclassify other opera's
historical reds. Missing `ci/reds` is a failure, not an early-return exemption.
In addition to existing per-behaviour command/exit/identity checks, require a
completed Node TAP test report for exactly the selected behaviour, with at
least one failed test diagnostic whose `code` is `ERR_ASSERTION` and whose
test name starts `W-096 behaviour N:` for that log's N. Parse TAP structure,
including its plan and diagnostic blocks; quoted examples and echoed command
text are not tests. The focused test must use Node's test/assert reporting
and select N from argv, so one aggregate failure cannot count as seven reds.
Reject missing/zero-test plans, arbitrary nonzero output, skipped-only runs,
module-load errors, SyntaxError, command-not-found, unknown flags and sandbox
or Git permission failures even alongside an assertion. A failing assertion
must execute existing code, not assert module availability. These checks
judge recognizable test output, not maliciously fabricated output under the
threat model above. Do not rewrite old logs into the new format.

W-096 also owns `opus.records_unchanged` (block) in the evidence rule module,
delegating comparison to a shared helper in commands' `opus-model.ts` so
verify and check use one implementation without a commands-to-CLI import.
The immutable baseline is **the reviewed trunk branch point**, not a signing
commit. The producer owns the D-021 sequence: branch first, then ready on the
owning opus branch. Extend the existing `bisellium branch W-096 --studio studio
--repo .` writer in `packages/cli/src/branch.ts` to resolve `origin/master`
once to an exact commit and cut `opus/W-096` there. Today's `createOpusBranch`
cuts from arbitrary HEAD and writes no baseline; it is not sufficient. For
W-096, require a clean checkout of master with HEAD equal to that resolved
ref and all trunk-side record changes landed; reject missing refs or an
existing opus branch before mutation. Create and check out the new owning
branch before writing its own record through the existing guarded
front-matter writer, setting `baseline_commit` once to that full
40-lowercase-hex branch-point id. Never write the pin on the trunk checkout
or another opus branch. Preserve existing non-W-096 branch behaviour. The
producer commits the command-produced pin on `opus/W-096` before `ready`;
`ready` then signs the spec gate and moves to building in its existing single
step. It neither writes the pin nor waits for a future signing commit.

W-096 alone receives a one-time producer bootstrap exception to the
requirement that its baseline pin precede ready. Its existing implementation
branch predates the baseline writer. The producer may now record
`baseline_commit: 0935d518d576f5e1cf81079d631b8b10a5d76742` on `opus/W-096`;
this records the verified historical branch point at the time of bootstrap
and does not claim that a pin existed before ready.

Implement the exception as a kept, tested one-off script,
`scripts/bootstrap-w096-baseline.ts`, using the same guarded front-matter
writer as `branch`. Before mutation, require the owning branch and real-studio
record, a clean checkout, an absent pin with no previous pin introduction in
record history, and a unique merge-base of HEAD and `origin/master` equal to
the full commit id above. Require that commit to be an ancestor of both refs
and require the existing protected-record comparison against it to pass for
HEAD and the working tree. Any failed prerequisite refuses without writing.
Write only the baseline field; do not recreate the branch, replay ready, alter
lifecycle dates, or rewrite existing evidence. Commit the producer's bootstrap
write before validation.

Thereafter check and verify apply the unchanged exact-commit, ancestry,
first-introduction, immutable-pin and protected-record checks. The bootstrap
grants no exemption from `opus.records_unchanged`, no recurring merge-base
inference, and no permission to bootstrap another opus or replace an existing
pin. All other retrospective pinning remains forbidden. This paragraph
supersedes the pre-ready bootstrap sequence solely for the existing W-096
branch. (Architect ruling, revision 7, 2026-10-01.)

`check` and `verify` resolve `baseline_commit` as an exact commit and require
it to be an ancestor of both `origin/master` and HEAD. A builder's later
non-trunk descendant containing changed records therefore cannot become a
valid baseline; changes already on trusted trunk have passed trunk review.
Compare the value with its first introduction in W-096's record history;
missing history or a changed pin is unverifiable. Missing/unresolvable trunk
refs or incomplete ancestry also fail closed. No rebase, merge, later
signature or integration-ref movement repins it; never use a current
merge-base or infer a baseline from a branch name.

The exact protected path set is the set at the baseline: all Git tree entries
matching `studio/opera/*.md` or `examples/sample-studio/opera/*.md` (direct
children only), minus `studio/opera/W-096.md`. Enumerate these paths from
that immutable tree, not a supplied checksum manifest or today's census.
Require regular files and compare their raw bytes and regular-file modes
against both HEAD and the working tree. Every protected record must remain
byte-identical; deletion, rename, symlink substitution or mode change fails.
Capture the full baseline object id and sorted protected path set in
command-produced validation output. The measured 101/7 table above is
historical context, never a required count.

Explicit exemptions are W-096's own record, whose authorized verification,
review and lifecycle/date bookkeeping must change normally, and new records
created by this opus's acceptance filings. Acceptance filings may create
new paths only; they cannot exempt or overwrite another baseline record.
New unrelated records, including records arriving through a rebase, are
outside the pinned set and do not alter it. All such records still undergo
ordinary model checks. No other baseline record is exempt.

Run these rules via check with repo context and W-096's verify/CI before
