## Acceptance

- Seven separate genuine reds exist before their respective production
  changes; the same selected assertions are green afterward. No aggregate
  failing run is presented as seven independently evidenced behaviours.
  `opus.red_assertion` blocks trivial evidence and is tested as part of this
  opus; no missing-import run or subsequently fabricated log counts.
- `npm test`, `npm run -s typecheck`, lint and format checks pass in the
  implementation environment. Run the actual kept build-plus-served command
  and retain its command-produced evidence; mocked gate success is insufficient.
- Check both real and sample officinae with the existing CLI, comparing
  findings to a captured pre-build baseline. No new blocking findings arise
  for unchanged records. Checks and tests leave the protected set at the baseline
  byte-identical, enforced by `opus.records_unchanged` against the producer's
  pinned branch-point commit, validated as an ancestor of origin/master and
  HEAD, with only the explicit own-record/acceptance-filing exemptions above;
  all negative cases live in temporary fixtures. Keep command-produced base
  identity/comparison evidence; a prose assertion of preservation is insufficient.
- Demonstrate UI readiness, served verification, missing-ruling done refusal,
  and successful done after a scoped Patron ruling in a temporary officina.
  Reopen and repeat to prove dates and source-bound UI evidence cannot survive
  a changed build as accidental approval. Repeat with only an in-place brief
  edit and with only a title edit to pin design-digest freshness. Demonstrate
  dissenting ui-lead input considered and cited by a passing censor, without
  requiring ui-lead assent. The censor remains the sole QA gate.
- The architect drafting environment cannot run Git: it does not run lifecycle
  evidence writers, claim recorded reds, sign a gate, commit, or certify a tree.
  Git-dependent implementation acceptance runs later in an authorized builder
  environment; sandbox failure must not be counted as behaviour evidence.

